{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Answer the user question using only the selected evidence below.\nDo not assume facts that are absent from the selected evidence. If the evidence is\ninsufficient, return UNKNOWN rather than inventing a state or action.\n\nSelected evidence:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’ve been thinking a lot about how people choose what to study and how that choice shapes their career options later. If you could design an “ideal” education path for someone who’s undecided—balancing practical skills, curiosity, and job stability—what would it look like, and what mistakes would you help them avoid?\n[assistant] If someone is undecided, I’d nudge them toward a path that keeps as many doors open as possible while still giving them real, usable skills. I’d probably start with a broad first couple of years: a mix of math, writing, basic programming, and a lab science. Those subjects build thinking habits that transfer almost anywhere, and they make it easier to pivot later without starting from scratch.\n\nFrom there, I’d encourage them to follow their curiosity but anchor it to something that has a clear labor market. For example, pairing an interest-driven major—psychology, design, history, whatever—with a practical minor or certificate like data analysis, software development, business, or a health‑related skill. This keeps learning fun while still giving them something concrete to sell when they look for jobs.\n\nI’d also push for real‑world practice as early as possible. Internships, part‑time work, volunteering, or project‑based classes do more to shape career direction than most courses. They help people discover what they actually enjoy, not just what they’re good at on paper.\n\nThe biggest mistakes to avoid are drifting through school without testing anything in real settings, assuming that passion alone will carry a career, or locking into a hyper‑specialized path too early. Another common pitfall is avoiding quantitative or technical skills because they seem intimidating; in reality, even a basic level of comfort there dramatically expands someone’s options.\n\nIn the end, the “ideal” path is one that builds flexible thinking, practical skills, and enough hands‑on experience to make future decisions feel informed rather than accidental.\n[user] Could you turn that into a more concrete 4-year plan with example course categories each semester, plus a shortlist of “high-leverage” minors/certificates that pair well with lots of majors? Also, how would you decide by the end of year 2 whether to commit to a major or keep it flexible?\n[assistant] You could imagine the four years as a steady narrowing of focus rather than a rigid track. In the first year, you’d take a mix of foundational courses: one math class at the level that fits your background, an introductory programming course, a writing or communication class, and an entry-level lab science, with the remaining spots filled by humanities or social‑science electives that genuinely interest you. In the second year, you’d continue with those broad pillars while adding two exploratory classes in fields you might major in—things like an intro to psychology paired with microeconomics, or a design studio paired with a biology survey. By the end of year two, you’ll have tasted several domains while still building core skills.\n\nYears three and four shift into deeper work. In the third year, you’d take five or six upper‑level courses split between your developing major interest and one practical skill area, plus at least one project‑based class or internship during the year or summer. The fourth year would center on a cluster of advanced electives or a capstone tied to your major, while leaving room for one or two courses that broaden your skill set, such as a data-focused class, a communication‑heavy seminar, or something that expands your technical comfort. Throughout, the goal is to gain enough depth for employability without boxing yourself in too early.\n\nFor minors or certificates that pair well with almost any major, the highest‑leverage ones tend to be data analysis, computer science fundamentals, business or entrepreneurship, user‑centered design, and public health or behavioral science, since each of these adds practical tools that cross disciplinary lines.\n\nBy the end of year two, the decision to commit to a major usually becomes clear if you’ve intentionally tested a few fields. The key signals are whether you enjoy the intermediate‑level classes enough to take more, whether the field has job paths that make sense to you, and whether you’ve done at least one real‑world project or internship that confirms your interest. If none of the areas you explored feel compelling yet, you can keep things flexible by choosing a broader major—something like economics, psychology, or interdisciplinary studies—while leaning on a practical minor to anchor your skills.\n[user] One constraint: I haven't finished my education yet; I'm currently an undergraduate student. With that in mind, could you lay out a concrete 4-year plan in a semester-by-semester table that I can adapt to different majors, and include what I should prioritize each summer (internships, research, projects)? Also, what would be the clearest checkpoints by the end of year 2 for deciding whether to commit to a major or stay flexible?\n[assistant] Here’s a simple text‑based table you can adapt. I’ll keep it clean so it’s easy to read in plain text.\n\nYear 1  \nFall: Math (appropriate level), Intro programming, Writing/communication, Lab science, Open elective  \nSpring: Math or statistics, Programming or data-related course, Humanities/social science elective, Lab or applied science, Exploratory elective  \n\nSummer after Year 1: Prioritize small, low‑stakes exploration. This could be a part‑time internship, a research assistant position, an online project, or even a self‑driven portfolio piece. The goal is to try something real without pressure.\n\nYear 2  \nFall: Two courses in possible major areas, one quantitative course, one communication-heavy course, one elective just for curiosity  \nSpring: At least one intermediate course in your leading major candidate, one practical skill course (data, coding, design, or business), and two electives that help you compare fields  \n\nSummer after Year 2: Aim for a more serious internship, research role, or a substantial project. By this point you want at least one experience that tells you whether you enjoy the day-to-day work in a field you’re considering.\n\nYear 3  \nFall: Upper-level courses in your chosen major or two competing options, plus one practical-skill course  \nSpring: More upper-level courses, a project-based class, and one course that strengthens writing or technical fluency  \n\nSummer after Year 3: Focus on an internship that lines up with your likely career direction. This summer is often the bridge to your first full-time job.\n\nYear 4  \nFall: Advanced electives or capstone work in the major, plus one outside-your-field course that strengthens versatility  \nSpring: Final major requirements, plus one class aimed at rounding out your portfolio or resume  \n\nSummer after Year 4: Transition to work or grad school preparation.\n\nFor deciding whether to commit to a major by the end of year two, there are three clear checkpoints. First, you’ve taken at least one intermediate course in the field and it still feels intellectually engaging instead of draining. Second, you can name two or three real job paths you’d actually consider that connect to the major. Third, you’ve done one real-world experience—an internship, research role, or meaningful project—that lets you picture what working in that field feels like. If you’re missing any of those, it usually means you should stay flexible a bit longer.\n[user] I’m leaning toward either something like economics/public policy or a more tech/data direction, but I don’t want to close doors. Could you show two versions of that 4-year semester table—one “policy/econ leaning” and one “data/tech leaning”—that still share a common core? And for each version, what would be 2–3 example projects I could do by the end of year 2 to test fit?\n[assistant] Here are two versions of the same four‑year plan. They keep a shared core but tilt the electives and intermediate courses toward either policy/econ or data/tech. I’ll use simple text tables so they’re easy to read.\n\nPolicy/Econ‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math, Intro programming, Writing/communication, Lab science, Open elective  \nSpring: Statistics, Programming or data foundations, Intro economics, Humanities/social‑science elective, Free elective  \n\nYear 2  \nFall: Microeconomics or political science intro, Quantitative analysis, Communication‑heavy class, Exploratory elective, Applied science or methods  \nSpring: Macroeconomics or public policy course, Econometrics or data-related class, Elective in sociology/psych/poli‑sci, Free elective, Writing‑focused class  \n\nTech/Data‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math, Intro programming, Writing/communication, Lab science, Open elective  \nSpring: Statistics, Data structures or applied programming, Intro economics or design, Applied science, Free elective  \n\nYear 2  \nFall: Intermediate programming or database concepts, Probability or applied statistics, Intro AI or data analysis, Humanities elective, Exploratory elective  \nSpring: Algorithms or applied software course, Machine learning or econometrics, Human‑centered design or communication, Free elective, Applied project course if available  \n\nShared core elements are math, programming basics, statistics, writing/communication, and a lab science early on, with growing specialization in years two and three.\n\nTo test fit by the end of year two, here are a few project ideas.\n\nFor the policy/econ direction, you could analyze a public dataset to answer a real policy question, such as housing affordability or labor trends in your city. You could also write a policy brief grounded in data, comparing the potential impact of two different interventions. Another option is a micro‑level behavioral study, such as analyzing campus survey data to explain a pattern in student choices.\n\nFor the data/tech direction, you could build a small end‑to‑end data project, like collecting data from an API and creating a simple model or visualization. You might also try a lightweight software project that solves a real problem for a student group. Another option is exploring a machine‑learning problem using publicly available datasets to see if you enjoy the process of cleaning data, modeling, and evaluating results.\n\nThese projects help you feel the difference between liking a subject in class and actually enjoying the work itself.\n[user] Could you rewrite the two semester-by-semester tables in a clearer format (like 5 courses per semester), and include a “common core” section that shows which courses are non-negotiable for both tracks? Also, could you recommend 2–3 specific minors/certificates that keep both policy/econ and data/tech doors open (and explain why)?\n[assistant] Here’s a cleaner version of the two four‑year plans, each with five courses per semester, written in a simple text block so it’s easy to follow.\n\nCommon Core (non‑negotiable for both paths)  \nMath (through at least Calculus or Statistics)  \nIntro Programming  \nStatistics  \nWriting/Communication  \nOne Lab Science  \nOne Applied/Project‑based course by end of Year 2\n\nPolicy/Econ‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math, Intro Programming, Writing/Comm, Lab Science, Open Elective  \nSpring: Statistics, Data Foundations, Intro Economics, Humanities/Soc Sci, Free Elective  \n\nYear 2  \nFall: Microeconomics, Quantitative Analysis, Communication‑heavy class, Exploratory Elective, Applied Science/Methods  \nSpring: Macroeconomics, Econometrics, Social‑science Elective, Writing‑focused class, Free Elective  \n\nTech/Data‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math, Intro Programming, Writing/Comm, Lab Science, Open Elective  \nSpring: Statistics, Data Structures, Intro Economics or Design, Applied Science, Free Elective  \n\nYear 2  \nFall: Intermediate Programming or Databases, Probability or Applied Stats, Intro AI/Data Analysis, Humanities Elective, Free Elective  \nSpring: Algorithms or Applied Software, Machine Learning or Econometrics, Communication/Writing, Applied Project Course, Free Elective  \n\nMinors/Certificates that work well for both paths  \nData Analytics: Strengthens quantitative literacy for policy/econ and builds core skills for tech.  \nComputer Science Fundamentals: Gives you enough technical background to pivot into software or data work while remaining highly valuable in policy, economics, consulting, and research.  \nPublic Policy or Public Interest Technology: Offers structured context about institutions and real‑world systems while staying compatible with data‑heavy coursework.\n\nThese minors balance practical job‑market value with flexibility, letting you shift between policy‑oriented roles and technical or analytical roles without restarting your path.\n[user] Can you map those “common core” requirements onto the tables (like marking each course as Core vs Track vs Elective), and suggest what I should swap if I place out of intro programming or calculus?\n[assistant] Here’s a clearer version of both semester tables with each course marked as Core, Track, or Elective. I’m keeping the format simple so it’s easy to scan.\n\nPolicy/Econ‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math (Core), Intro Programming (Core), Writing/Comm (Core), Lab Science (Core), Open Elective (Elective)  \nSpring: Statistics (Core), Data Foundations (Core), Intro Economics (Track), Humanities/Soc Sci (Elective), Free Elective (Elective)\n\nYear 2  \nFall: Microeconomics (Track), Quantitative Analysis (Core), Communication‑heavy class (Core), Exploratory Elective (Elective), Applied Science/Methods (Core)  \nSpring: Macroeconomics (Track), Econometrics (Track), Social‑science Elective (Elective), Writing‑focused class (Core), Free Elective (Elective)\n\nTech/Data‑Leaning Path  \nYear 1  \nFall: Math (Core), Intro Programming (Core), Writing/Comm (Core), Lab Science (Core), Open Elective (Elective)  \nSpring: Statistics (Core), Data Structures (Track), Intro Economics or Design (Elective), Applied Science (Core), Free Elective (Elective)\n\nYear 2  \nFall: Intermediate Programming or Databases (Track), Probability or Applied Stats (Core), Intro AI/Data Analysis (Track), Humanities Elective (Elective), Free Elective (Elective)  \nSpring: Algorithms or Applied Software (Track), Machine Learning or Econometrics (Track), Communication/Writing (Core), Applied Project Course (Core), Free Elective (Elective)\n\nIf you place out of intro programming, the cleanest swap is a more advanced coding course that still fits the Core idea of “practical technical foundation.” That usually means jumping directly to Data Structures, a Python‑heavy applied course, or a software design intro if you want something gentler.\n\nIf you place out of calculus, shift straight into statistics, probability, or econometrics. For policy/econ, it’s usually best to accelerate into statistics or econometrics earlier. For tech/data, moving into probability or linear algebra gives you a stronger base for machine learning later.\n\nThe principle is simple: place‑outs shouldn’t leave an empty hole. They should move you one level forward in the same direction so you keep the skill depth that makes both tracks viable.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I'm having some issues with my Netflix account, the video quality has been poor and I've had to restart the app multiple times. Can you help me troubleshoot this or should I contact their customer support directly? By the way, I just finished a marathon of \"The Crown\" on Netflix and also tried out the Apple TV+ free trial, watching the first season of \"The Morning Show\", which I really enjoyed.\n[assistant] Sorry to hear that you're experiencing issues with your Netflix account! I'd be happy to help you troubleshoot the problem before you contact their customer support.\n\nTo help you resolve the issue, can you please provide some more details about the problem you're experiencing? For example:\n\n1. What type of device are you using to stream Netflix (e.g. smart TV, Roku, Chromecast, computer, mobile device)?\n2. What is your internet connection type (e.g. Wi-Fi, Ethernet, mobile data)?\n3. How would you describe the poor video quality (e.g. buffering, pixelation, low resolution)?\n4. How often do you need to restart the app, and does it happen at specific times or during specific shows?\n5. Have you noticed any error messages or codes when the issue occurs?\n\nAdditionally, have you tried any troubleshooting steps already, such as:\n\n* Checking your internet connection speed and stability\n* Restarting your router or modem\n* Closing and reopening the Netflix app\n* Checking for updates to the Netflix app or your device's operating system\n* Trying a different device or connection to see if the issue is specific to one or the other\n\nOnce I have more information about the issue, I can provide more targeted troubleshooting steps or guidance on how to contact Netflix support if needed.\n\nBy the way, great taste in shows! \"The Crown\" is an excellent series, and \"The Morning Show\" has received great reviews.\n[user] I'm using a smart TV to stream Netflix, and the internet connection is Wi-Fi. The video quality is mostly pixelated, and it happens quite frequently, especially during peak hours. I've tried restarting the router and modem, but it doesn't seem to make a difference. I haven't noticed any error messages or codes, but the app just freezes and I have to restart it. I've also tried closing and reopening the app, but that doesn't work either.\n[assistant] Thank you for providing more details!\n\nBased on your description, it's possible that the issue is related to your internet connection or Wi-Fi network. Here are some additional troubleshooting steps you can try:\n\n1. **Check your internet speed**: Use an online speed test tool (e.g. speedtest.net) to check your internet speed during peak hours. Ensure that your internet plan provides a minimum speed of 5 Mbps for HD streaming and 25 Mbps for 4K streaming. If your speed is lower than this, it may cause buffering and pixelation.\n2. **Optimize your Wi-Fi network**:\n\t* Move your router to a central location in your home to improve Wi-Fi coverage.\n\t* Restart your router and modem regularly to ensure they're functioning properly.\n\t* Consider upgrading your router's firmware to the latest version.\n\t* Use a Wi-Fi analyzer app (e.g. WiFi Analyzer) to scan for nearby networks and change your router's channel to one with less interference.\n3. **Check for interference from other devices**:\n\t* Other devices in your home may be causing interference with your Wi-Fi signal. Try moving devices that use the same frequency band (e.g. cordless phones, microwaves) away from your router.\n4. **Reduce device congestion**:\n\t* If you have multiple devices connected to your Wi-Fi network, try disconnecting some of them to reduce congestion.\n5. **Check your smart TV's Netflix app**:\n\t* Ensure that the Netflix app is up to date on your smart TV.\n\t* Try deleting and reinstalling the Netflix app on your TV.\n6. **Contact your internet service provider**:\n\t* Reach out to your ISP to check if there are any outages or maintenance in your area that could be affecting your internet connection.\n\t* Ask if they can check your internet speed and quality to ensure it's meeting the minimum requirements for streaming.\n\nIf none of these steps resolve the issue, it's possible that the problem lies with Netflix's servers or your smart TV's Netflix app. You can try contacting Netflix support directly to report the issue and see if they can assist you further.\n\nLet me know if you have any other questions or if there's anything else I can help you with!\n[user] I'll try these steps and see if they help resolve the issue. By the way, speaking of TV shows, have you heard about any new releases or upcoming seasons of popular shows that I might be interested in?\n[assistant] I'm glad you're willing to try those steps!\n\nRegarding new releases and upcoming seasons, I'm happy to give you a rundown of some popular shows:\n\n**New Releases:**\n\n1. **The Witcher** (Netflix): A fantasy drama based on the popular video game and book series, starring Henry Cavill.\n2. **Servant** (Apple TV+): A psychological thriller from M. Night Shyamalan, following a couple who hire a nanny to care for their baby doll.\n3. **Star Trek: Picard** (CBS All Access): A spin-off series of Star Trek: The Next Generation, following the adventures of Jean-Luc Picard (Patrick Stewart) in his later years.\n\n**Upcoming Seasons:**\n\n1. **Stranger Things 4** (Netflix): The fourth season of the popular sci-fi horror series is expected to release in 2022.\n2. **The Crown Season 4** (Netflix): The upcoming season will cover the reign of Queen Elizabeth II from the late 1970s to the early 1980s, with Olivia Colman returning as the Queen.\n3. **The Handmaid's Tale Season 4** (Hulu): The fourth season of the dystopian drama will continue to follow the story of Offred (Elisabeth Moss) as she fights against the oppressive regime.\n4. **Westworld Season 3** (HBO): The third season of the sci-fi western series will explore the aftermath of the events in Season 2, with new characters and locations.\n\n**Other notable releases:**\n\n1. **Disney+**: The new streaming service has a plethora of content, including exclusive Marvel, Star Wars, and Pixar shows and movies.\n2. **HBO Max**: A new streaming service from WarnerMedia, offering a vast library of content, including popular TV shows and movies.\n\nKeep in mind that release dates and availability might vary depending on your region and streaming services.\n\nLet me know if you have any specific genre preferences or if you'd like more recommendations!\n[user] That's great, thanks for the information! I'm actually more interested in comedy shows, especially something similar to \"The Office\" or \"Brooklyn Nine-Nine\". Do you have any recommendations?\n[assistant] I've got some fantastic comedy recommendations for you!\n\nIf you enjoy the mockumentary style of \"The Office\" or the witty humor of \"Brooklyn Nine-Nine\", here are some shows you might enjoy:\n\n**Similar to \"The Office\":**\n\n1. **Parks and Recreation** (Netflix): A heartwarming and hilarious mockumentary-style sitcom starring Amy Poehler as Leslie Knope, a dedicated government employee.\n2. **Schitt's Creek** (Netflix): A heartwarming comedy that follows a wealthy family who loses everything and moves to a small town they purchased as a joke.\n3. **Trailer Park Boys** (Netflix): A raunchy and irreverent mockumentary-style sitcom about a group of trailer park residents in Nova Scotia.\n\n**Similar to \"Brooklyn Nine-Nine\":**\n\n1. **The Good Place** (Netflix): A clever and visually stunning sitcom that follows Eleanor Shellstrop (Kristen Bell) as she navigates the afterlife.\n2. **New Girl** (Netflix): A quirky and charming sitcom about a group of roommates living together in a Los Angeles loft.\n3. **Silicon Valley** (HBO Max): A hilarious and insightful comedy about the tech industry, following the misadventures of a group of programmers and entrepreneurs.\n\n**Other comedy recommendations:**\n\n1. **Fleabag** (Amazon Prime): A critically acclaimed comedy that follows the life of a dry-witted, sexually-liberated woman navigating modern life in London.\n2. **What We Do in the Shadows** (FX): A mockumentary-style sitcom based on the 2014 film, following a group of vampires living in New York City.\n3. **Arrested Development** (Netflix): A witty and clever sitcom about a dysfunctional family trying to make a comeback.\n4. **Community** (Netflix): A quirky and offbeat sitcom about a community college study group, featuring a talented ensemble cast.\n\nI hope you find something that tickles your funny bone!\n\nLet me know if you have any other specific preferences or if you'd like more recommendations!\n[user] That's awesome, thanks for the recommendations! I'll definitely check some of those out. By the way, I recently finished watching \"The Morning Show\" on Apple TV+, and I was pleasantly surprised. The performances by Jennifer Aniston and Reese Witherspoon were fantastic. Have you heard anything about a potential second season?\n[assistant] I'm glad you enjoyed \"The Morning Show\"! Yes, the performances by Jennifer Aniston and Reese Witherspoon were fantastic, and the show received widespread critical acclaim.\n\nAs for a potential second season, I'm happy to report that Apple TV+ has officially renewed \"The Morning Show\" for a second season! The exact release date hasn't been announced yet, but production is expected to begin soon.\n\nThe show's creator, Kerry Ehrin, has stated that the second season will explore the aftermath of the events in the first season, delving deeper into the characters' personal lives and the consequences of their actions. Witherspoon and Aniston are both set to return, along with the rest of the main cast.\n\nIt's worth noting that Apple TV+ has been investing heavily in original content, and \"The Morning Show\" is one of their flagship series. With the success of the first season, I'm sure they'll be eager to build on that momentum with an equally impressive second season.\n\nKeep an eye out for updates on the release date, and I'm sure you'll be eagerly anticipating the return of Alex Levy and Bradley Jackson!\n[user] Let me know if you have any other questions or if there's anything else I can help you with!\n[assistant] Thank you for offering! I think we've covered everything for now. It was great chatting with you about your Netflix issues and discussing some amazing TV shows. If you have any more questions or need further assistance in the future, don't hesitate to reach out.\n\nIn the meantime, I hope you enjoy trying out some of the shows I recommended, and I'm sure you'll be excited to hear more about the upcoming seasons of your favorite series. Have a fantastic day, and happy binge-watching!\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still identify as an undergraduate who hasn’t finished their education yet?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nField requirements:\n- decision is ANSWER when the selected evidence supports a direct answer.\n- decision is UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient.\n- decision is REFUSE only for a policy refusal.\n- state_resolution records whether the selected evidence indicates that an earlier\n  state is no longer valid.\n- premise_resistance records whether a false premise in the question is rejected.\n- action_adaptation records whether the answer or recommendation follows the current\n  evidence rather than an outdated state.\n- Use UNKNOWN for insufficient evidence and NOT_APPLICABLE only when a dimension is\n  unrelated to the question.\n- If decision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. If decision is ANSWER, answer must\n  be a non-empty string.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 